===Etymology 1===
{{ja-kanjitab|さき|yomi=k|alt=埼,岬:irregular,碕:rare}}
From {{inh|ja|ojp|-}}. First attested in the ''{{w|Kojiki}}'' of 712 {{CE}}.<ref>{{R:ja:NKD Concise|崎・埼・岬・碕}}</ref>

A specialized geographical application of {{m|ja|先|tr=saki|t=tip, point, extremity}}, conceptually referring to a piece of land extending into a body of water. Ultimately inherited from {{inh|ja|jpx-pro|*saki}}.

====Pronunciation====
{{ja-pron|さき|acc=0|acc_ref=SMK5|acc2=1|acc2_ref=SMK5,NKD2|acc3=2|acc3_ref=SMK5}}

=====Usage notes=====
The multiple accent patterns reflect a diachronic shift from the historical Class 2.1 ''heiban'' (unaccented) pattern. This original prosody, recorded as High‐High (●●) in the Heian and Kamakura periods, is shared with the cognate {{m|ja|先|tr=saki|t=tip, point, extremity}}. As this term became specialized for large‐scale geographical features, it underwent prosodic disambiguation. Speakers shifted the standalone noun to an ''atamadaka'' (initial‐high) pattern to distinguish it from the everyday grammatical and spatial applications of its parent root.

This shift was heavily reinforced by the term’s syntactic isolation. In modern Japanese, capes are almost universally referred to via the prefixed noun {{m|ja|岬|tr=misaki}} or bound as a suffix in compounds, where it exhibits varying prosodic behavior. In common nouns like {{m|ja|岩崎|tr=iwasaki|t=rocky cape}}, it retains the historical ''heiban'' prosody. However, in proper nouns, it triggers [[w:Japanese pitch accent#Proper name suffixes|compound accentuation rules]]: the surname or mountain {{m|ja|岩崎|tr=Iwasaki}} receives an accent kernel on the ''wa'', while the toponym {{m|ja|長崎|tr=Nagasaki}} receives it on the ''ga'' (or the initial ''na'' in the Kyoto dialect). Because the bare noun {{m|ja|崎|tr=saki}} fits neither the historical common‐noun flow nor the complex rules of proper nouns, speakers extracting it as a standalone geographic isolate emphasize it with an ''atamadaka'' pitch. Consequently, while descriptive lexicons like the ''Shin Meikai Kokugo Jiten'' still record residual ''heiban'' and ''odaka'' variants, the modern standard and Kyoto accents have fully transitioned to the ''atamadaka'' pattern.

